\begin{enumerate}
    \item The set of even functions is defined as functions $f(x)$ such that $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x$.
    \item The set of odd functions is defined as functions $f(x)$ such that $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x$.
    \item We are looking for the number of functions that are both even and odd. This means that $f(-x) = f(x)$ and $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x$.
    \item Let $f(x)$ be a function such that $f(-x) = f(x)$ and $f(-x) = -f(x)$.
    \item Then $f(x) = -f(x)$, which implies $2f(x) = 0$, so $f(x) = 0$ for all $x$.
    \item The only function that satisfies both conditions is $f(x) = 0$.
    \item Thus, there is only one function that is both even and odd.
\end{enumerate}

